## Rational tuning of thorium–organic frameworks by reticular chemistry for boosting radionuclide sequestration
Han Feng§, Xiaohong Xiong§, Lele Gong, Huiping Zhang, Ying Xu, Xuefeng Feng, and Feng Luo (✉)
School of Chemistry, Biology and Materials Science, East China University of Technology, Nanchang 330013, China § Han Feng and Xiaohong Xiong contributed equally to this work.
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2021 Received: 14 April 2021 / Revised: 19 May 2021 / Accepted: 16 June 2021
## ABSTRACT
The reticular chemistry strategy presents a powerful molecule-design tool to tailor the physical and chemical properties of metal–organic framework (MOF). In this work, we for the first time investigated the effect of organic ligands on the radionuclide sequestration \((\mathsf{T c O}_{4}{}^{-})\) of thorium–organic framework. Through a coordination modulation technique, two novel isoreticular thorium–organic frameworks, namely Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68, were obtained. Relative to the antetype MOF of Th-MOF-66 that shows extremely low uptake of \(\mathsf{R e O}_{4}{}^{-}\) (a chemical surrogate of radioactive \(\mathsf{T c O}_{4}{}^{-}\)), the isoreticular MOFs of Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68 enable ultrahigh uptake of \(\mathsf{R e O}_{4}{}^{-}\), giving an impressively 36.8-fold or 56-fold enhancement, respectively. The adsorption capacity of Th-MOF-68 is as high as \(560\;\mathrm{mg/g}\), exceeding most reported adsorbents for such use. The mechanism for such exceptional outstanding performance, as unveiled by both the single crystal X-ray diffraction and theoretical calculation, is due to coordination interaction for Th-MOF-67, when a tetrazolate ligand was used, or a combined effect from both coordination interaction and anion-exchange for Th-MOF-68, if using a triazolate ligand.
## KEYWORDS
thorium–organic framework, ligand effect, defect site, anion exchange, radionuclide sequestration
## 1 Introduction
Eliminating oxometal anionic pollutants has emerged as a serious issue and a challenging task, due to its long-term damage to environment and human health resulted from their high environmental mobility and a fact that the major natural minerals are not available adsorbent in light of their inherent neutral or negative charge in the skeleton [1, 2]. Among these oxometal anionic pollutants, the research of removing perrhenate \((\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-})\) is highly interesting and desirable [3]. First, rhenium (Re) is not only one of the rarest elements in the Earth, but also a key ingredient for modern industry in superalloy, catalysis, and spaceflight [4]. Thereby, \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\), as the major source of Re, its regeneration extracted from environment, ore, and oxometal anionic pollutants, is highly valuable. Second, \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\), showing a similar structure and almost identical charge density with \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}{}^{-}\), is generally used as a nonradioactive surrogate to replace radioactive \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}{}^{-}\) for research, from the safe viewpoint [5]. As we know, \(^{99}\mathrm{Tc}\) is a key radioactive species in the nuclear fuel cycle, showing a very long half-life up to 213,000 years [6]. As estimated in 2017 from the International Atomic Energy Agency, the total inventory of \(^{99}\mathrm{Tc}\) is up to \(154,500~\mathrm{kg}\) from nuclear power plant, let alone the additional \(^{99}\mathrm{Tc}\) pollutants from nuclear accidents and nuclear waste and weapon tests [7]. Generally, \(^{99}\mathrm{Tc}\) in environment renders the stable chemical species of \(^{99}\mathrm{TcO}_{4}{}^{-}\), a typical oxometal anionic pollutants if without the consideration of its high radioactivity.In principle, eliminating oxometal anionic pollutants can be achieved by the cationic framework materials through the electrostatic anion exchange. In this regard, traditional anion-exchange materials such as layered double hydroxides (LDHs) [8], lanthanide hydroxide (rare-earth hydroxides (LRHs)) [9], and thorium borates (Notre Dame thorium borate-1 (NDTB-1)) [10, 11] were tested. However, very low adsorption capacity was often observed in this species, which without doubt seriously restricts their practical application. On the other hand, traditional commercialized polymeric anion-exchange resins can also remove \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}{}^{-}\) [12], however, which are inherently restricted by their weak chemical stability. Thereby, there is an urgent need to exploit new cationic framework materials with both high chemical stability and large adsorption capacity.Cationic organic cages or organic-metal cages present a newly developed category for this target [13–16]. The inherent pocket can selectively recognize \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) via hydrogen-bond interactions, thus affording high selectivity towards \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) [17, 18]. However, this class, as solid adsorbents, is still blocked by their low chemical stability and adsorption capacity.The impressive cationic framework materials are cationic metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [19–31]. The design principle is based on the coordination of transition metal ions with neutral multidentate nitrogen-donor ligands [32–45]. For example, SCU-100, constructed from \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\) and neutral tetradentate nitrogen-donor ligands, shows ultrahigh \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) uptake of \(541~\mathrm{mg/g}\) [33], while the self-assembly of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) with the same ligands produces SCU-101 [32] and SCU-102 [37] that give \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) uptake of \(217~\mathrm{mg/g}\) and \(291~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. In another case, TJNU-302, a \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\)-based cationic MOF, affords \(211~\mathrm{mg/g}\) uptake of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) [39]. In a Cu-bpy-CBs three-membered system
In principle, eliminating oxometal anionic pollutants can be achieved by the cationic framework materials through the
electrostatic anion exchange. In this regard, traditional anionexchange materials such as layered double hydroxides (LDHs) [8], lanthanide hydroxide (rare-earth hydroxides (LRHs)) [9], and thorium borates (Notre Dame thorium borate-1 (NDTB-1)) [10, 11] were tested. However, very low adsorption capacity was often observed in this species, which without doubt seriously restricts their practical application. On the other hand, traditional commercialized polymeric anion-exchange resins can also remove \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}{}^{-}\) [12], however, which are inherently restricted by their weak chemical stability. Thereby, there is an urgent need to exploit new cationic framework materials with both high chemical stability and large adsorption capacity.
Cationic organic cages or organic-metal cages present a newly developed category for this target [13–16]. The inherent pocket can selectively recognize \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) via hydrogen-bond interactions, thus affording high selectivity towards \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) [17, 18]. However, this class, as solid adsorbents, is still blocked by their low chemical stability and adsorption capacity.
The impressive cationic framework materials are cationic metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [19–31]. The design principle is based on the coordination of transition metal ions with neutral multidentate nitrogen-donor ligands [32–45]. For example, SCU-100, constructed from \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\) and neutral tetradentate nitrogendonor ligands, shows ultrahigh \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) uptake of \(541~\mathrm{mg/g}\) [33], while the self-assembly of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) with the same ligands produces SCU-101 [32] and SCU-102 [37] that give \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) uptake of \(217~\mathrm{mg/g}\) and \(291~\mathrm{mg/g}\) , respectively. In another case, TJNU302, a \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\) -based cationic MOF, affords \(211~\mathrm{mg/g}\) uptake of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}\) [39].  In  a  Cu-bpy-CBs  three-membered  system
(SCP-IHEP-1), \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) uptake of \(211~\mathrm{mg/g}\) was observed [38]. Wang et al. reported a rare case (SCU-103) showing effective \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) removal at alkaline environment with adsorption capacity of \(318~\mathrm{mg/g}\) [43]. Impressively, this class often shows high crystallization, making acquiring the structure information of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) after anion exchange feasible by means of the single crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD). However, the stability of MOFs is still a serious issue, as most MOFs are based on divalent metal cations.
Moreover, the cationic covalent organic frameworks (COFs) recently emerged to be an effective alternative, as these materials show not only high \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) adsorption capacity, but also inherent high chemical stability [46–52]. For example, cationic COFs like that of SCU-COF-1 [49], SCU-CPN-1 [48], PQAPN(Me)2Py-Cl [50], and \(\mathrm{[C_{2}VimBr]_{286}}\) -TbDa-COF [51] afford \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) uptake up to 702, 999, 1,127, and \(952~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. However, the expensive cost from organic substrate remains a serious restriction of their practical application, while deep insight into the exact host-guest interactions is still unclear due to the inherent weak crystallization of COFs.
Accordingly, it is suggested that cationic framework with high chemical stability and high crystallization could be the ideal adsorbent for \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) removal. Generally speaking, materials with high chemical stability typically are based on higher-valent metal ions or organic linkers with increased basicity such as triazolate or tetrazolate groups. In the case of the former, MOF materials of UiO-66 or similar isoreticular compounds, are based on high valence metal center of \(Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})\), and show considerable promise with high thermal, chemical, and hydrolytic stability. However, obtaining single crystal remains a big challenge for this species, due to the easy-to-hydrolysis nature of \(Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})\) ion [53]. But this can be rationally solved by replacing \(Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})\) with comparable higher-valent metal ion of Th(IV) that affords relatively less hydrolysis nature [54, 55]. In the case of the later, since \(Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})\) ions are preferred to coordinate with carboxylate linkers, the synthesis of \(Z\mathrm{r}\) -MOFs containing azolate-coordinated linkers remains a big challenge.
To this end, in this work we focus on the synthesis of Th-MOFs to explore the ligand effect on the radionuclide sequestration through a coordination modulation technique from carboxylate linkers to tetrazolate- and then triazolate-containing linkers. Th-MOF-66, showing isoreticular structure with UiO-66, was selected as the model structure, in light of its high chemical stability and crystallization [56]. The use of azolate-containing linkers is expected to tailor the coordination surrounding and consequently charge distribution of Th cluster \(\mathrm{(Th_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{6})}\), thus boosting its radionuclide sequestration performance. Moreover, the use of triazolate-containing linker is expected to construct cationic Th-MOF for anion-exchange with \(\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}\).
