## 3.1 Structures of Th-MOF-66/67/68
\(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) cluster connects to 12 organic ligands \((\mathrm{bdc}^{2-}\), or \(\mathrm{teba}^{2-}\), or \(\mathrm{trba}^{-}\)), constructing the overall UiO-66-type three-dimension framework.
## 3.2 Stability, porosity, and surface charge of Th-MOFs
The thermal stability of these MOFs was initially investigated by thermogravimetric (TG) analysis. Since the thermal stability of Th-MOF-66 and Th-MOF-67 has been reported [56, 57], only Th-MOF-68 was tested (Fig. S4 in the ESM). Before \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the major loss was solvent molecules. Then, a \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\)-exchange method was employed to activate Th-MOF-68. The TG test showed that the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\)-exchanged samples rendered a platform at \(120{-}257\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and accordingly, the activated temperature was set as \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The thermal stability test, as evidenced by temperature-dependent PXRD patterns, suggested its high thermal stability up to \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S5 in the ESM). The chemical stability of the crystal samples after immersing in water, water solution with \(\mathrm{pH}=1\) and 3, and alkaline NaOH solution \((1\,\mathrm{M})\) for three days was also confirmed by PXRD tests and corresponding photograph of crystal samples (Figs. S5 and S6 in the ESM). The permanent porosity of Th-MOF-68 was attested by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\), giving a typical type I profile (Fig. S7 in the ESM), suggesting microporous material of Th-MOF-78. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and pore volume were \(486\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and \(0.25\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\), and a narrow pore size distribution at \(0.78\,\mathrm{nm}\) was observed, smaller than the estimated aperture \((1.0\,\mathrm{nm})\) from structure analysis, mainly due to the occupation of \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)ions within the pore.
To disclose the surface charge in these Th-MOFs, the zeta potential test was carried out. As shown in Fig. 2, Th-MOF-66 and Th-MOF-67 displayed almost neutralized surface zeta potentials of 1.15 and \(1.78\,\mathrm{mV}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,5.0\), confirming their neutral net, in well agreement with the crystal structure, while Th-MOF-68 displayed clearly positive surface zeta potentials of \(44\,\mathrm{mV}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,5.0\), strongly suggesting cationic framework, also well consistent with the crystal structure.
## 3.3 Research of radionuclide sequestration
The above success in the synthesis of azolate-coordinated Th-MOFs and then the observation of ligand effect on the surface charge of Th-MOFs further arouse us to investigate the
application for radionuclide sequestration. \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}^{-}\)is a typical radioactive species from the nuclear fuel cycle. Thus, from the consideration of safety, nonradioactive \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)was used as a surrogate to replace radioactive \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}^{-}\), when we carried out the research of \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}^{-}\), because both of them own high similarity in charge densities and chemical properties. First, the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)uptake experiment was performed by mixing \(10\,\mathrm{mg}\) of Th-MOF-68 with \(20\,\mathrm{mL}\) solution containing \(200\,\mathrm{ppm}\,\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{ReO}_{4}\). Then a rapid adsorption was observed and a short sorption equilibrium within \(100\,\mathrm{min}\) was reached (Fig. 3(a)). The value is much shorter than that observed in some cationic frameworks such as SLUG-21 [42] and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{3}^{+}Cl^{-}}\) [40] (within \(24\,\mathrm{h}\)). After contacting for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\), high removal efficiency up to \(82.4\%\) from the initial \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)solution \((200\,\mathrm{ppm})\) was observed. The fast sorption kinetics is mainly due to the porosity and regular pore in Th-MOF-68. The rate constant \((k_{2})\) can be obtained by fitting the pseudo-second-order model, giving \(0.5\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{g\cdot mg^{-1}\cdot min^{-1}}\) (Fig. S8 and Table S1 in the ESM), confirming fast sorption kinetics.
The adsorption capacity was explored for a \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{ReO}_{4}\) solution with concentration of \(100{-}500\,\mathrm{ppm}\) under a contact time of \(100\,\mathrm{min}\). Ultrahigh \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)uptake up to \(560\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) was observed (Fig. 3(b)). This value is higher than most cationic materials (Fig. 3(c) and Table S2 in the ESM), including in commercial anion-exchange resins such as purolite A532E \((446\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [58] and D318 resin \((351\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [59], inorganic materials such as LDHs \((130\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [8], \(\mathrm{YPbOClO}_{4}\)-1 \((430\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [9], composite materials of \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\mathrm{rGO}\) \((43.5\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [60], GO-DEA-DIBA \((140.8\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [61], and MOFs like that of UiO-66-NH\(_{3}^{+}\)Cl\(^{-}\) \((159\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [40], TJNU-302 \((211\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [38], SCP-IHEP-1 \((211\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [37], SCU-101 \((217\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [31], SCU-102 \((291\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [36], and SCU-100 \((541\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [32].
The adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)in cationic framework of Th-MOF-68 is reasonable, due to anion exchange between \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\), which will give the theoretic adsorption capacity of \(403\,\mathrm{mg/g}\); however, the value is far less than the experimental value of \(560\,\mathrm{mg/g}\), implying additional adsorption except for anion exchange. In this regard, we further explored the adsorption of \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{ReO}_{4}\) upon neutral framework of Th-MOF-67. Th-MOF-67 is theoretically expected to give no adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\), due to its neutral nature. However, this MOF shows impressively faster sorption kinetics with shorter sorption equilibrium within \(30\,\mathrm{min}\), relative to the cationic framework of Th-MOF-68, thus creating bigger rate constant \((k_{2})\) of \(2.3\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{g\cdot mg^{-1}\cdot min^{-1}}\) (Fig. S8 in the ESM). Noticeably, the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)adsorption capacity of neutral framework of Th-MOF-67 is
also as high as \(368~\mathrm{mg/g}\) (Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S9 in the ESM), also exceeding most cationic MOFs such as \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{3}^{+}Cl^{-}}\) \((159\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) [40], TJNU-302 \((211~\mathrm{mg/g})\) [38], SCP-IHEP-1 \((211~\mathrm{mg/g})\) [37], SCU-101 \((217~\mathrm{mg/g})\) [31], and SCU-102 \((291~\mathrm{mg/g})\) [36]. The results indicate additional adsorption beyond anion exchange for these Th-MOFs. To clarify this, we further explored the \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) adsorption upon the neutral framework of Th-MOF-66. However, Th-MOF-66 gives almost no adsorption (extremely low adsorption capacity of \(10~\mathrm{mg/g}\), Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S10 in the ESM).
The above results suggest strong ligand effect on the radionuclide sequestration of \(\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}\) for these Th-MOFs. To make clear the ligand effect, the characterization of the optical microscopy and scanning electron microscopy-energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (SEM-EDS) was initially tested. High chemical and mechanical stability even after adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) was observed for both Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68, meeting the demand for practical application (Fig. S11 in the ESM). The EDS results show uniform distribution of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) in the samples of Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68 after \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) adsorption (Fig. S12 in the ESM). The anion exchange for cationic framework of Th-MOF-68 can be directly reflected on the absence of \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) element in EDS spectra of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) -loaded samples. The adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) can be further confirmed by the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectra, where the characteristic vibration of Re–O bond of \(908~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for ThMOF-68 and \(917~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for Th-MOF-67 was observed (Fig. S13 in the ESM) [14, 15]. The stability after \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) adsorption was confirmed by PXRD test (Figs. S14 and S15 in the ESM).
## 3.4 Adsorption mechanism
To obtain the exact structure information of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) within MOFs, we further determined the crystal structure of Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68 after \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) adsorption. Thanks to the high crystallization, chemical and mechanical stability, the crystals of both Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68 even after loading \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) were well maintained. Single crystal XRD disclosed that the
\(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) -anchored samples (defined as Th-MOF-67-Re and ThMOF-68-Re) also crystallized in the cubic crystal system with \(F m3m\) space group. The unit cell is identical to Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68. The overall structure is the same as observed in Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68. In Th-MOF-67-Re, the \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) was impressively coordinated to Th cluster with Th–O bond length of 2.50 (14) \(\mathring\mathrm{A}\) (Fig. 4(a)), which means that the adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) for neutral framework of Th-MOF-67 is resulted from the replacement of terminally coordinated water molecules by \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) through coordination interaction. The Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\right)\) bond length is shorter than the corresponding Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond length of 2.60 (18) \(\mathring\mathrm{A}\), suggesting stronger affinity for this \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster with \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) ions. Accordingly, given that each \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster in Th-MOF-67 can theoretically coordinate with 6 \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) ions, then a theoretical adsorption capacity of \(385~\mathrm{mg/g}\) is expected, which is well consistent with the experimental value of \(368~\mathrm{mg/g}\).
Th-MOF-68-Re shows similar coordinated \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\) ions ending with the \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster (Fig. 4(b)). The Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}\right)\) bond length of 2.51 (4) \(\mathring\mathrm{A}\) is comparable with that observed in
Th-MOF-67-Re. Besides, free-standing \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) ions around \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster were also observed in Th-MOF-68-Re (Fig. 4(b)), corresponding to anion exchange, which were stabilized by strong hydrogen bonds with very short \(\mathrm{O}^{\bullet\bullet}\mathrm{O}\) contact of ca. \(2.3\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) between \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) ions and the \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) units of \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster. Given that in Th-MOF-68 each \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster can not only theoretically coordinate with \(6~\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) ions, but also theoretically afford hydrogen bond with \(4~\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) ions, a theoretical adsorption capacity for Th-MOF-68 is \(609\;\mathrm{mg/g},\) which is in well agreement with the experimental value of \(560\;\mathrm{mg/g}\).
The above results confirm that the adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) on neutral framework of Th-MOF-67 is due to coordination interaction between \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) ion and \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster through the terminally coordinated water molecule, while for the cationic framework of Th-MOF-68, except for similar coordination interaction, anion exchange stabilized by hydrogen bonds was also observed. Note that these Th-MOFs of Th-MOF-66, ThMOF-67, and Th-MOF-68 are isoreticular net and contain the same \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster, and thus comparable \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) adsorption for Th-MOF-66 can be deduced. However, our experiment results show almost no \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) adsorption for neutral framework of Th-MOF-66. The difference among Th-MOF-66, Th-MOF-67, and Th-MOF-68 is the organic linkers, for example carboxylate linkers in Th-MOF-66 and azolate-containing linkers in Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68. Thus, significant ligand effect could be resulted from the Th–O bond \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) terminally coordinated water molecule of \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) cluster) when carboxylate or azolate-containing linkers are used.
To illuminate the ligand effect, we further carried out the density functional theory (DFT) calculation with the simplified structures of Th-MOF-66-c and Th-MOF-67-c, and Th-MOF-68-c (Fig. S16 in the ESM). Figure S17 in the ESM describes the charge density for those three structures. To discover more detail about Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond, we also calculated the density of state (DOS) to obtain the bond information, as shown in Fig. 5. It is well known that the intensity of bonding interaction is reflected in the orbital position energy and overlapping area in DOS. For Th-MOF-66-c, there are two overlapping region at \(-6.0\) and \(-3.0\;\mathrm{eV}\) for Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond, indicating strong Th–O bond in Th-MOF-66. By comparison, there is only one overlapping region at \(-3.0\;\mathrm{eV}\) for ThMOF-67-c, strongly suggesting relatively weaker Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond in Th-MOF-67. In Th-MOF-68-c the overlapping region crossing the Fermi level \(\left(E_{\mathrm{F}}=0.0\;\mathrm{eV}\right)\) was observed, indicating relatively weaker Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond in Th-MOF-68. Accordingly, the bond strength of Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond in those Th-MOFs follows the sequence, Th-MOF-66 > Th-MOF-67 > Th-MOF-68, implying significant ligand effect.
Further, we calculated the binding energy of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) with Th-MOFs based on the simplified structures of Th-MOF-66-c, Th-MOF-67-c, and Th-MOF-68-c. First, for Th-MOF-66, the
Further, we calculated the binding energy of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) with Th-MOFs based on the simplified structures of Th-MOF-66-c, Th-MOF-67-c, and Th-MOF-68-c. First, for Th-MOF-66, the adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) on this neutral framework through coordination interaction (defined as process I) shows the binding energy of \(+0.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. S18 in the ESM); this presents an unfavorable thermodynamic process, and no adsorption is expected, which is well consistent with the experimental results (extremely low adsorption capacity of \(10\;\mathrm{mg/g}\)). This is mainly because of the strong Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond in Th-MOF-66, as evidenced by DOS calculation, which consequently leads to difficult replacement of coordinated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) by \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\). By contrast, the neutral tetrazole-containing framework of Th-MOF-67 and the cationic triazole-containing framework of Th-MOF-68 give the binding energy of \(-1.4\) and \(-0.41\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. S18 in the ESM), respectively, through process I; this suggests that the adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) on Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68 through coordination interactions is thermodynamically spontaneous. And strong chemical adsorption is expected, which is also well consistent with the experimental results. The reason, as evidenced in the DOS calculation, is mainly due to the relatively weaker Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond in Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68 induced by ligand effect, which consequently leads to the easy breakage of Th–O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond and then accessible replacement by \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\). Moreover, for the cationic framework of Th-MOF-68, except for process I, the binding energy through anion exchange (defined as process II) is calculated to be \(-0.87\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. S19 in the ESM); this also presents a favorable thermodynamic process (Fig. S19 in the ESM). And additional adsorption of \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) from anion exchange is expected, giving the theoretical adsorption capacity of \(244\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) through process II. This, in conjunction with process I, gives the total theoretical adsorption capacity of \(609\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) for Th-MOF-68, which agrees well with the experimental value of \(560\;\mathrm{mg/g}\). Moreover, we also found that the adsorption through process I can not be desorbed, whereas adsorption through process II shows good recycle use (Fig. S20 in the ESM).
## 3.5 Extending to UiO-66
To explore the metal effect on the \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) adsorption, we further carried out the \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) adsorption on UiO-66 samples (a \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\)-based analog for Th-MOF-66, Fig. S1 in the ESM). The adsorption isotherm of UiO-66 is similar to Th-MOF-66, and very low adsorption capacity of \(12\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) was observed, suggesting no metal effect on the \(\mathrm{ReO_{4}^{-}}\) adsorption. Moreover, we further explored the modification of UiO-66 by azole ligand as currently employed for Th-MOF-66. However, we failed to synthesize the target product, suggesting somewhat metal effect on the synthesis of these special MOFs.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, we demonstrate herein the proof-of-concept of ligand effect on the radionuclide sequestration of Th-MOFs.
To increase the basicity of organic linkers, azolate linkers of tetrazole- and triazole-containing linkers were used, and two novel azolate-based Th-MOFs (Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68) were obtained. Significant ligand effect on the radionuclide sequestration of \(\mathrm{TcO}_{4}^{-}\) was observed among the antetype MOF of Th-MOF-66 and the modified isoreticular MOFs of Th-MOF-67 and Th-MOF-68, leading to a 36.8-fold and 56-fold enhancement in the radionuclide sequestration performance. The adsorption mechanism was clearly disclosed by both single crystal XRD and theoretical calculation, due to coordination interaction in Th-MOF-67 and a combined operation from both coordination interaction and anion exchange in Th-MOF-68. The results show that the ligand effect through increasing the basicity of organic linkers (such as tetrazolate and triazolate linkers) can significantly weaken the Th-O \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) bond, thus creating defect site for binding with guest molecules such as \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\) anions observed in this work. It is also believed that this unique ligand effect will be a powerful tool to create defect site for broad applications such as anion contamination, separation and catalysis.
